  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "Positive numbers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) satisfy \\(x+y=10\\). What is the maximum value of \\(xy\\)?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "25",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: AM-GM with a fixed sum\n\nSince \\(x,y>0\\), AM-GM bounds the product using the fixed sum:\n\\[\nxy\\le\\left(\\frac{x+y}{2}\\right)^2=25.\n\\]\nThe product is greatest when \\(x=y=5\\), and its maximum value is \\(5\\times 5=25\\).\n\nMethod 2: Complete the square\n\nThe fixed sum lets us write \\(y=10-x\\), with \\(0<x<10\\) because both numbers are positive. Completing the square expresses the product as a constant minus a square:\n\\[\nxy=x(10-x)=25-(x-5)^2\\le25.\n\\]\nThe square is zero at \\(x=5\\), giving \\(y=5\\). This pair is allowed, so the maximum is \\(25\\).",
